#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082858 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f0aabf-5329-59e7-a5e8-6db979b8699c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Displacement"}] :question/id #uuid "297f1978-f735-52d2-810f-78acd4d35766" :question/math-academy-id "e-1488" :question/problem "A penny is projected vertically downward with an initial velocity of $300 \\, \\textrm{m/min}$ and hits the ground after $2$ seconds. Find the distance the penny covered during its fall." :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- We can find the initial velocity of the object in meters per second as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =300\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =300\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =300\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{300}{60}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}} \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The penny falls for $t=2\\,\\textrm{s}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\  & =(5)(2)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(2)^{2} \\\\  & =10+19.6 \\\\  & =29.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $29.6\\,\\textrm{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082859 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d441155e-dbcb-598d-afc7-949ee4eddbee" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Time"}] :question/id #uuid "03a61226-750f-5227-9887-04b31184f5bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-1490" :question/problem "A stone is projected vertically downward from a height of $19.6\\,\\textrm{m}$ with a velocity of  $14.7\\,\\textrm{m/s}.$ How long will it take for the stone to hit the ground?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the stone is $u=14.7\\,\\textrm{m/s}.$\n\n- The stone covers the distance of $s=19.6\\,\\textrm{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}19.6 & =(14.7)t+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8){t}^{2} \\\\ 4(4.9) & =3(4.9)t+(4.9){t}^{2} \\\\ 4\\cancel{(4.9)} & =3\\cancel{(4.9)}t+\\cancel{(4.9)}{t}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =3t+{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+3t-4 & =0 \\\\ (t+4)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-4,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the stone hits the ground after $t=1$ second."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083055 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c3fb8a8-343a-5046-afce-83db17ec1039" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Initial Velocity"}] :question/id #uuid "8bddae46-ab34-5d0d-b9d3-a02de1a2a72c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9738" :question/problem "A ball is projected vertically down from a building with a height of $110.4 \\, \\textrm{m}.$ It takes $4\\,\\textrm{s}$ to hit the ground. What was the initial velocity of the ball?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The ball falls for $t=4\\,\\textrm{s}.$\n\n- The ball covers the distance of $s=110.4\\, \\textrm{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ (110.4) & =u(4)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(4)^{2} \\\\ 110.4 & =4u+78.4 \\\\ 32 & =4u \\\\ u & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the ball was $u= 8 \\, \\textrm{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d441155e-dbcb-598d-afc7-949ee4eddbee" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59a8f98c-2b2f-5534-ac78-ca0d47cfe098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302880} {:answer/id #uuid "b35e2565-7c0a-5110-9457-574120881750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302881} {:answer/id #uuid "b68e841b-69c2-578e-aad5-451e730a25df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302882} {:answer/id #uuid "46f841d8-ff18-5fe7-83b2-05261a6e788f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302883} {:answer/id #uuid "509a053d-901a-533b-9795-5bf37bd07397" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b68e841b-69c2-578e-aad5-451e730a25df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302882} :answer-field/id #uuid "8aa3b800-b3d4-5e34-8f5a-392ee6ed9fb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2086c80-ba1e-5c33-a529-5c1e9cecd9e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-10510" :question/problem "A ball is dropped vertically downward from a height of $44.1\\,\\text{m}.$ How long does it take for the ball to hit the ground?\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the ball is $u=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$\n\n- The ball covers the distance of $s=44.1\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ 44.1 & =(0)t+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8){t}^{2} \\\\ 44.1 & =4.9{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{44.1}{4.9} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =9 \\\\ t & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the ball hits the ground after $t=3$ seconds."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f0aabf-5329-59e7-a5e8-6db979b8699c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "892c87d5-354f-5a9b-b160-fd34961f006b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44.1m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079689} {:answer/id #uuid "8b849248-9358-563f-acb7-a7eb013fef92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29.4m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079690} {:answer/id #uuid "a966bd08-cf6f-5561-998b-6740f6e16629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.7m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079691} {:answer/id #uuid "f7149d03-1e4e-5c97-b17b-0d190b702855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.08m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079692} {:answer/id #uuid "4774f044-702c-5dec-8766-01eac8f9c4f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "88.2m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "892c87d5-354f-5a9b-b160-fd34961f006b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44.1m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079689} :answer-field/id #uuid "222539fa-2a37-5fca-b50a-9d938dacd614" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e3ddfe5-6a0f-5dd6-97e4-c3a65ef84d31" :question/math-academy-id "q-10586" :question/problem "A ball is dropped from a height and hits the ground in $3$ seconds. Find the distance the ball covered during its fall. \n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the ball is $u=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$\n\n- The ball falls for $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\  & =(0)(3)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(3)^{2} \\\\  & =0+4.9(9) \\\\  & =44.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $44.1\\,\\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f0aabf-5329-59e7-a5e8-6db979b8699c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8225847-f244-5cd7-b62b-841916cfb99c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "270.0\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302887} {:answer/id #uuid "9afa2353-734a-5a25-aeae-b86c5e3c0346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "147.5\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302888} {:answer/id #uuid "6735c49f-9e77-5d95-8e3c-25787b289872" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "74.0\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302889} {:answer/id #uuid "eac4c568-8bd5-55b5-83e8-8c7e9bd72d3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49.5\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302890} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f8bc03-08b9-5e5e-a164-74253586ea0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "205.5\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9afa2353-734a-5a25-aeae-b86c5e3c0346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "147.5\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302888} :answer-field/id #uuid "79c27fe7-19b3-59a9-a5a7-c3f904afa217" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0759bd1-4ce9-5754-8e66-728af4ee921b" :question/math-academy-id "q-135099" :question/problem "A marble is projected vertically downward from a building with a velocity of $0.3\\,\\text{km/min}.$  It takes the marble $5\\,\\text{s}$ to hit the ground. What is the height of the building? \n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- We can find the initial velocity of the marble in meters per second as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =0.3\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =0.3\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =0.3\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =0.3\\cdot \\frac{1000\\,\\text{m}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The marble falls for $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\  & =(5)(5)+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (9.8)(5)^{2} \\\\  & =25+(4.9)(25) \\\\  & =147.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the building is $147.5\\,\\text{m}$ high."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c3fb8a8-343a-5046-afce-83db17ec1039" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Initial Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2ad088b-3f36-58e2-8f6d-a1931be99e87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302894} {:answer/id #uuid "604a099c-8ede-5be4-bbe7-bdecd1d32d6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302895} {:answer/id #uuid "b195ede5-efe9-5fcf-8657-3bc3ee345200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302896} {:answer/id #uuid "df3a572e-5683-5464-bdeb-416c16cbe6e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302897} {:answer/id #uuid "699b4e9c-259b-50dd-a763-2e8404ee1531" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b195ede5-efe9-5fcf-8657-3bc3ee345200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302896} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa8fb2cc-746e-5448-b296-10b684672a35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "805cd1c7-4904-5a21-8004-de671d029b88" :question/math-academy-id "q-135104" :question/problem "An apple is projected vertically down from a height of $0.08\\,\\text{km}$ and hits the ground in $4\\,\\text{s}.$ What was the initial velocity of the apple?\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The apple falls for $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- We can find the distance the apple falls in meters as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =0.08\\,\\text{km} \\\\  & =0.08\\,\\text{km}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{km}} \\\\  & =0.08\\,\\cancel{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}} \\\\  & =0.08\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =80\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ (80) & =u(4)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(4)^{2} \\\\ 80 & =4u+78.4 \\\\ 1.6 & =4u \\\\ u & =0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the apple was $u=0.4\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c3fb8a8-343a-5046-afce-83db17ec1039" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Initial Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b30d21be-9a64-571d-a492-a75569401209" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61.2\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302901} {:answer/id #uuid "9f5de20b-1fc1-5012-95fd-f39fc5275f61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80.2\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302902} {:answer/id #uuid "27e54da7-a097-52b4-bb23-332b2010317e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "67\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302903} {:answer/id #uuid "f040569b-3f52-5268-9c1d-9246fa5296bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75.1\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302904} {:answer/id #uuid "562a65ba-be43-52af-bee1-1afa2c05f5ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70.6\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "562a65ba-be43-52af-bee1-1afa2c05f5ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70.6\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302905} :answer-field/id #uuid "81534138-97f5-5bb3-9f19-548720685555" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04947b95-8a1f-567f-948f-2de5de86130e" :question/math-academy-id "q-135105" :question/problem "An object is projected vertically down from a height of $0.6\\,\\text{km}$ and hits the ground in $6\\,\\text{s}.$ What was the initial velocity of the object?\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The object falls for $t=6\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- We can find the distance the object falls in meters as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =0.6\\,\\text{km} \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\text{km}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{km}} \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\cancel{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}} \\\\  & =0.6\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =600\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ (600) & =u(6)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(6)^{2} \\\\ 600 & =6u+176.4 \\\\ 423.6 & =6u \\\\ u & =70.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the object was $u=70.6\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c3fb8a8-343a-5046-afce-83db17ec1039" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Initial Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15fc59e3-0dbf-53f1-8e46-c0d37301684c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.1\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302908} {:answer/id #uuid "1e49e830-c63f-5747-8b14-bc97b2987f1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.6\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302909} {:answer/id #uuid "7f422857-f73f-5d06-9669-bfcdccd16c24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.2\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302910} {:answer/id #uuid "4262f712-c4a8-54d1-b044-f5a00ac5bba8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.4\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302911} {:answer/id #uuid "e5bb10b1-895b-5cef-8e65-23d6a190a3f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5bb10b1-895b-5cef-8e65-23d6a190a3f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302912} :answer-field/id #uuid "c670b444-9c5a-5118-9955-566fc9f3647a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7301eaa9-795f-5260-bdb1-f81e61dd3675" :question/math-academy-id "q-135223" :question/problem "A coin is projected vertically down from a skyscraper with a height of $720\\,\\text{m}.$ It takes $12\\,\\text{s}$ to hit the ground. What was the initial velocity of the coin?\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The coin falls for $t=12\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- The coin covers the distance of $s=720\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ (720) & =u(12)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(12)^{2} \\\\ 720 & =12u+705.6 \\\\ 14.4 & =12u \\\\ u & =1.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the coin was $1.2\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302913 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d441155e-dbcb-598d-afc7-949ee4eddbee" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d34d7b5-5110-5f2d-995e-c5cf6357acbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302915} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ad1762-195b-56b2-a679-5386328dd586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302916} {:answer/id #uuid "a90df014-bd33-5b6e-adfd-33187474bce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302917} {:answer/id #uuid "80bcd42a-7b00-5a41-a10b-e4a656ab076b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302918} {:answer/id #uuid "71228a3f-b65a-5a43-974f-a9e3ff20edca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a90df014-bd33-5b6e-adfd-33187474bce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302917} :answer-field/id #uuid "38788958-6a78-590e-9c37-e97ece947ee5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "183a141f-bd68-52b7-ae90-0aa68787bc11" :question/math-academy-id "q-135228" :question/problem "A marble is projected vertically downward from a height of $245\\,\\text{m}$ with an initial velocity of $24.5\\,\\text{m/s}.$ How long will it take for the marble to hit the ground? \n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the marble is $u=24.5\\,\\text{m/s}.$\n\n- The marble covers the distance of $s=245\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ 245 & =(24.5)t+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8){t}^{2} \\\\ 245 & =24.5t+4.9{t}^{2} \\\\ 50(4.9) & =5(4.9)t+4.9{t}^{2} \\\\ 50\\cancel{(4.9)} & =5\\cancel{(4.9)}t+\\cancel{(4.9)}{t}^{2} \\\\ 50 & =5t+{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+5t-50 & =0 \\\\ (t+10)(t-5) & =0 \\\\ t & =-10,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the marble hits the ground after $t=5$ seconds."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f0aabf-5329-59e7-a5e8-6db979b8699c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21da426d-ff73-55ce-bfe8-e76bbfa25fac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "138.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21da426d-ff73-55ce-bfe8-e76bbfa25fac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "138.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232632} :answer-field/id #uuid "547064b1-e32c-58cd-bf58-e1617027a4dc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dac6a978-302a-5e0d-9924-081014bd2660" :question/math-academy-id "q-221581" :question/problem "An object is projected vertically downward from a building with a velocity of $0.9\\,\\text{km/min}.$ It takes the object $4\\,\\text{s}$ to hit the ground. What is the height of the building? **Do not round your answer**.\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*\n\nThe height of the building is{{field-1}}$\\text{m}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- We can find the initial velocity of the object in meters per second as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =0.9\\cdot \\frac{1000\\,\\text{m}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =15\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The object falls for $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\  & =(15)(4)+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (9.8)(4)^{2} \\\\  & =60+(9.8)(8) \\\\  & =138.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the building is $138.4\\,\\text{m}$ high."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d441155e-dbcb-598d-afc7-949ee4eddbee" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0005c0f2-d6ad-5066-be83-5e32dfc03ecc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0005c0f2-d6ad-5066-be83-5e32dfc03ecc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302922} :answer-field/id #uuid "b62247f6-bd0f-5a4a-a971-b0ec2d4835e8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41edcb77-220b-5a30-9ef7-eb64ca48aac7" :question/math-academy-id "q-221596" :question/problem "An object is dropped vertically downward from a height of $313.6\\,\\text{m}.$ How long does it take for the object to hit the ground?\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*\n\nThe object hits the ground in{{field-1}}seconds." :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the object is $u=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$\n\n- The object covers the distance of $s=313.6\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ 313.6 & =(0)t+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8){t}^{2} \\\\ 313.6 & =4.9{t}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{313.6}{4.9} & ={t}^{2} \\\\ 64 & ={t}^{2} \\\\ t & =\\pm 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the object hits the ground after $t=8$ seconds."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f0aabf-5329-59e7-a5e8-6db979b8699c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64d86ea9-1540-5352-94f9-5a12031f0751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64d86ea9-1540-5352-94f9-5a12031f0751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302925} :answer-field/id #uuid "8113d7e0-1d86-5fc1-893d-41d839e35485" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45dc4bf8-eed9-521c-9175-662faf86f5f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-251480" :question/problem "An apple is dropped from a height and hits the ground in $0.05$ minutes. Find the distance the apple covered during its fall. **Do not round your answer**.\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*\n\nDuring its fall, the apple covered a distance of\n{{field-1}}$\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the apple is $u=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$\n\n- We can find the time it takes for the apple to fall in seconds as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =0.05\\,\\text{min} \\\\  & =0.05\\,\\text{min}\\cdot \\frac{60\\,\\text{s}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =0.05\\,\\cancel{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{60\\,\\text{s}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}} \\\\  & =3\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 3+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9.8\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =0+4.9\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =44.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance covered by the apple is $44.1\\,\\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f0aabf-5329-59e7-a5e8-6db979b8699c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b50a3ac6-1033-5c42-b49b-75b4ccd6dd8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b50a3ac6-1033-5c42-b49b-75b4ccd6dd8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302928} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c2019f8-04e3-54da-8d10-8d9ce08239f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8b5e608-2200-5223-b086-a18df00bb813" :question/math-academy-id "q-251483" :question/problem "A billiard ball is projected vertically downward with an initial velocity of $0.25\\,\\text{m/s}$ and hits the ground after $0.025$ minutes. Find the distance the billiard ball covered during its fall. **Do not round your answer**.\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*\n\nDuring its fall, the billiard ball covered a distance of\n{{field-1}}$\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the billiard ball is $u=0.25\\,\\text{m/s}.$\n\n- We can find the time it takes for the billiard ball to fall in seconds as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =0.025\\,\\text{min} \\\\  & =0.025\\,\\text{min}\\cdot \\frac{60\\,\\text{s}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =0.025\\,\\cancel{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{60\\,\\text{s}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}} \\\\  & =1.5\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.25)(1.5)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(1.5)^{2} \\\\  & =0.375+11.025 \\\\  & =11.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $11.4\\,\\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c3fb8a8-343a-5046-afce-83db17ec1039" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Initial Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e1cf6ed-39e5-58fd-b65b-40b6d02b58bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e1cf6ed-39e5-58fd-b65b-40b6d02b58bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302931} :answer-field/id #uuid "04941cbf-3bd7-5a54-ba23-49ec1dfc88a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c74668ee-26b0-5adf-a3cb-6d4db7a860f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-251595" :question/problem "A pear is projected vertically down from a height of $1.5\\,\\text{m}.$ It takes $0.5\\,\\text{s}$ to hit the ground. What was the initial velocity of the pear? **Do not round your answer**.\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*\n\nThe initial velocity was{{field-1}}$\\text{m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The pear falls for $t=0.5\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- The pear covers the distance of $s=1.5\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ (1.5) & =u(0.5)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(0.5)^{2} \\\\ 1.5 & =0.5u+1.225 \\\\ 0.275 & =0.5u \\\\ u & =0.55\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the pear was $0.55\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c3fb8a8-343a-5046-afce-83db17ec1039" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Initial Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "782922f8-a5b1-5bd8-9b87-9200c5878647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "782922f8-a5b1-5bd8-9b87-9200c5878647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302934} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef2022cf-c5ee-5a72-92f2-e6d32d2f9822" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9955871-7a2b-5833-a349-093c6e6531aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-251707" :question/problem "A ball is projected vertically down from a height of $4.5\\,\\text{km}$ and hits the ground in $30\\,\\text{s}.$ What was the initial velocity of the ball?\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*\n\nThe initial velocity was\n{{field-1}}$\\text{m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The ball falls for $t=30\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- We can find the distance the ball falls in meters as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =4.5\\,\\text{km} \\\\  & =4.5\\,\\text{km}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{km}} \\\\  & =4.5\\,\\cancel{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}} \\\\  & =4.5\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =4\\,500\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ (4\\,500) & =u(30)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(30)^{2} \\\\ 4\\,500 & =30u+4\\,410 \\\\ 90 & =30u \\\\ u & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the ball was  $u=3\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d441155e-dbcb-598d-afc7-949ee4eddbee" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbe28e4a-fccb-57f6-85e9-8cf93b6cd87e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302937} {:answer/id #uuid "53516ac7-1295-5dbc-ba12-76cd5f29e4da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302938} {:answer/id #uuid "e3601c99-1db7-52e7-83fb-8f4a5fd101fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302939} {:answer/id #uuid "fd476e8d-6269-570a-9eea-4613747ce6c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302940} {:answer/id #uuid "59be3aca-dbf7-55ab-8948-c1cf91e2aa95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53516ac7-1295-5dbc-ba12-76cd5f29e4da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302938} :answer-field/id #uuid "c638cad5-5250-5ab1-a5ba-d64db75a6f71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4de376bd-96ce-5105-b48d-6021ccb45d98" :question/math-academy-id "q-31409" :question/problem "A brick is dropped vertically downward from a height of $176.4\\,\\text{m}.$ How long does it take for the brick to hit the ground?\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the brick is $u=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$\n\n- The brick covers the distance of $s=176.4\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ 176.4 & =(0)t+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8){t}^{2} \\\\ 176.4 & =4.9{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{176.4}{4.9} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =36 \\\\ t & =\\pm 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the brick hits the ground after $t=6$ seconds."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d441155e-dbcb-598d-afc7-949ee4eddbee" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3a894f9-4256-556b-a6e2-d3f67fc25f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.26\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302944} {:answer/id #uuid "c31a4c43-b104-5208-a79f-e7f98649f40d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.56\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302945} {:answer/id #uuid "2776e057-338c-5bb3-8171-ef42c0cb1539" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.83\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302946} {:answer/id #uuid "2fb66cde-8e11-5a03-8b31-349335cad391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.53\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302947} {:answer/id #uuid "d33171c2-edb7-5dbc-83d2-66077fff321d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.32\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2776e057-338c-5bb3-8171-ef42c0cb1539" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.83\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302946} :answer-field/id #uuid "c49fa83e-beed-596e-96f7-f02661044ac3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bd7ba8c8-a475-5dfc-9236-81e389c6fb25" :question/math-academy-id "q-31411" :question/problem "A marble is projected vertically downward from a height of $88.2\\,\\text{m}$ with a velocity of  $141.12\\,\\text{km/h}.$ How long will it take for the marble to hit the ground? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places. \n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- We can find the initial velocity of the marble in meters per second as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =141.12\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}} \\\\  & =141.12\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}\\cdot \\frac{1000\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{h}}{60\\,\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =141.12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}\\cdot \\frac{1000\\,\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{60\\,\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =141.12\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{{60}^{2}\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =39.2\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The marble covers the distance of $s=88.2\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}88.2 & =(39.2)t+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8){t}^{2} \\\\ 18(4.9) & =8(4.9)t+(4.9){t}^{2} \\\\ 18\\cancel{(4.9)} & =8\\cancel{(4.9)}t+\\cancel{(4.9)}{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+8t-18 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{{8}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-18)}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{64+72}}{2} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{-8\\pm 11.6619}{2} \\\\  & ≈-9.83,\\,1.83.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the marble hits the ground after $1.83\\,\\text{s}.$"}]}
